A||N||11||johnmac||vacuum lines inquiry||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||10-27-2000||10:08 PM||||Were can I find the plastic tubings for the vacuum lines of my 86GT?<P>And how do I test the vacuum on the lines?<P>Thanks<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-27-2000||10:24 PM||||I got new rubber vacuum lines at Pep Boys.  Any parts place has either prepackaged vacuum lines (6 feet) in a bubble pack or buy it by the foot. There are different size vacuum lines.  I've found two different size lines on my 87.||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||10-27-2000||10:57 PM||||you can get the hard line at allot of parts stores. I got some at advance auto awhile back. hard part to find is the rubber connectors.<P>to form the new line soak it in hot water. hold it in place untill it cools.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-28-2000||12:53 AM||||If you are talking about the plastic lines that run under the upper intake plemun, then <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>Rodney Dickman</A> sells the 8pc. kit to replace them all with steel lines. You use the old rubber connectors and put them on the ends of his new lines.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-28-2000||01:51 PM||||If the rubber ends are bad see if they are in the MotorMite Master Catalog at your local parts place. It might be that they make them but most places just don't stock them. There's all kinds of things in that book. Allot of them you'd think you could only get at a dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||10-28-2000||04:56 PM||||I just finnished installing Rodney Dickman's steel line kit.  I highly recommend it if your plastic lines are going bad.  Its pretty involved to replace them so don't bother replaceing plastic with plastic, unless you really never to save a few bucks.  I painted mine with red engine paint.  Looks really sharp and matches the manifold.||||reg||10
Z||000006||dogtired||10-30-2000||05:09 AM||||I also have one of Rodneys kits and I highly Recomend it.<BR>I chased a vacuum leak for almost 2 years trying to get my idle under control. I installed Rodneys Kit and a NEW EGR tube and things couldn't be better    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>GOOD Luck||||reg||6
Z||000007||86_Fastback||10-30-2000||08:28 AM||||dogtired <P>Where did you get your EGR tube?<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||10-30-2000||08:34 AM||||EGR tube are still available throug GM. I got one earlier this year for $86+ tax. FOCOA also has them for$79||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-30-2000||05:37 PM||||I would reccomend getting a flexable one (I think this is the one FOCOA sells), this way there is no chance of it freezing and cracking.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jhonny||11-02-2000||10:00 AM||||Is it possible to use ordinary off the shelf rubber vacuum hoses instead off the plastic ones ?<BR>Any drawbacks except for normal rubber ageing ?<P>/// Jhonny||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||11-03-2000||05:49 PM||||Yes you can use regular rubber vacuum tubing.||||reg||1
